---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import SendLab from "../../components/SendLab.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import SourceNote from "../../components/SourceNote.astro";
import SourcePanel from "../../components/SourcePanel.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomUrl, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

// The question-first format (DECISIONS.md D21.1, D29.1): the question, a plain
// answer, the figure, then the code in Source notes. Links to the code live in
// the notes only.
const lang = "en";
const r = room("send-receive");
const TR = "module/zfs/dmu_traverse.c";
const SEND = "module/zfs/dmu_send.c";
const RECV = "module/zfs/dmu_recv.c";
const L = {
  fromtxg: gh(SEND, 2370),
  minTxg: gh(TR, 653),
  flags: gh(SEND, 2088),
  getBirth: gh(TR, 78, 84),
  birthComment: gh("include/sys/spa.h", 483, 488),
  holesDefault: gh(TR, 44),
  zeroHoles: gh(TR, 249, 276),
  holeFile: gh(SEND, 1018, 1034),
  holeMeta: gh(SEND, 1012, 1017),
  dumpFree: gh(SEND, 367, 403),
  dnodeRange: gh(SEND, 1098, 1108),
  dnodeLoop: gh(TR, 534, 543),
  overlay: gh("include/sys/zfs_ioctl.h", 374, 387),
  payloadSum: gh(SEND, 295, 298),
  end: gh(SEND, 2619, 2626),
  recvBegin: gh(RECV, 1304, 1306),
  recvSum: gh(RECV, 2849, 2851),
  queue: gh(RECV, 3500, 3513),
  endCheck: gh(RECV, 3000, 3007),
  cleanup: gh(RECV, 3582, 3588),
  loop: gh(RECV, 463, 464),
  guids: gh(SEND, 2049, 2050),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="send-receive"
  title="How does ZFS send only what changed? send / receive · Uberblok"
  description="zfs send finds what changed between two snapshots from the txg each block was born in, skips unchanged subtrees without reading them, and writes a stream whose every record the receiver checks before applying it. Interactive, with the OpenZFS code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)} · {r.title.en}</p>
      <h1>How does ZFS send only what changed?</h1>
      <div class="prose answer">
        <p class="lead">
          To keep a copy of a filesystem on another machine, a file-by-file tool such as rsync has to find what changed
          since the last copy. It lists or reads every file on both sides, every time, and it sees a change only through
          the dates, sizes or contents it compares. On a large filesystem where little has changed, almost all of that
          work finds nothing.
        </p>
        <p>
          ZFS does not compare files. Each pointer to a block records the transaction group, or txg, in which the block
          was written. A txg is a batch of changes committed to disk together, and its number only grows. A snapshot
          taken in txg F holds no block written after F, so what changed between two snapshots is the blocks of the
          newer one written after the older one's txg. When ZFS changes a block, it also writes new copies of every block
          above it, up to the root. A block with an old txg therefore has nothing new below it. An incremental send walks
          the newer snapshot from the top and skips each block older than F, with everything below it, without reading
          it.
        </p>
        <p>
          What the walk finds becomes a stream of records: a WRITE for each block of data, a FREE for each range that
          was emptied, an OBJECT for each file. Each record carries a checksum of all the stream before it, and the
          receiving side checks that checksum before it applies the record. A stream damaged on the way stops at the
          first bad record, and the receive is undone. An incremental stream also names the snapshot it starts from,
          and the receiver refuses it unless it holds that snapshot.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="lab">
      <h2 id="lab"><span class="n">1</span> Send @s4, from the snapshot you choose</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The filesystem <code>tank/fs</code> has four snapshots, taken in txgs 10, 20, 30 and 40 (the history above
          the tree says what each txg changed). The figure sends the last one, <code>@s4</code>. On the left is its tree,
          each block pointer with the txg its block was born in. In the middle is the stream, and on the right the
          receiving side.
        </p>
        <p>
          Choose None first, for a full stream, and press Next step a few times. F is 0, so every pointer is visited and
          every block travels: 21 records, 11,672 bytes. Then choose <code>@s3</code>, which the receiver already holds,
          and step again. A pointer born at or before txg 30 turns dashed, the blocks below it fade, and they are never
          read: 7 records, 2,184 bytes. Each step outlines what it did, first the pointer it tested and the records it
          wrote, then the record the receiver checked and the blocks that record changed. Flip a bit to damage the
          stream on the way, and the receiver stops at the next header.
        </p>
      </div>
      <SendLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="code">
      <h2 id="code">In the code</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        The figure follows six places in <code>dmu_send.c</code>, <code>dmu_traverse.c</code> and
        <code>dmu_recv.c</code>. Open a Source note to see the lines, taken from release zfs-2.4.4.
      </p>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Where the walk starts</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>send_traverse_thread</code> starts the walk of the snapshot being sent. Its lower bound is
          <code>fromtxg</code>, the txg in which the from-snapshot was taken, or 0 for a full stream. The figure calls
          it F.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>send_traverse_thread</code>, dmu_send.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={SEND}
            from={1211}
            to={1213}
            symbol="send_traverse_thread"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 1211,
                to: 1212,
                text: `<code>fromtxg</code> is <a href="${L.fromtxg}">the from-snapshot's creation txg</a>, 0 for a full stream. Inside the traversal it becomes <a href="${L.minTxg}"><code>td_min_txg</code></a>.`,
              },
              {
                from: 1213,
                text: `<code>TRAVERSE_LOGICAL</code> joins <a href="${L.flags}">the send's flags</a>, and every block the traversal visits goes to <code>send_cb</code>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>The test that skips a subtree</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>traverse_visitbp</code> runs for every block pointer the walk reaches, and one comparison decides. A
          block born at or before the bound was already in the from-snapshot. The function returns without reading it,
          so nothing below it is reached either. That is safe because <a href={roomUrl(lang, "copy-on-write")}>copy-on-write</a>
          gives a new birth to every block above a change.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>traverse_visitbp</code>, dmu_traverse.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={TR}
            from={277}
            to={279}
            symbol="traverse_visitbp"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 277,
                text: `With <code>TRAVERSE_LOGICAL</code>, <code>get_birth_time</code> <a href="${L.getBirth}">returns the logical birth</a>, the txg in which the data was written. <a href="${L.birthComment}">The comment that defines the two births</a> says the physical one differs for dedup, block cloning and rewritten blocks, and that the logical one is the one for incremental replication.`,
              },
              {
                from: 278,
                text: `Returning here skips the block and its whole subtree: in the figure the pointer turns dashed and what is below it fades. A hole of birth 0 takes <a href="${L.zeroHoles}">the branch above</a> and is visited, as <code>send_holes_without_birth_time</code> is <a href="${L.holesDefault}">1 by default</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>From blocks to records</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>send_cb</code> gets every block the walk visits. A block of data becomes a WRITE and a hole becomes a
          FREE. In a visited dnode block, each file's dnode (the record that describes the file) becomes an OBJECT,
          changed or not, and each empty slot becomes FREEOBJECTS. That is why sending <code>@s4</code> from <code>@s3</code> still sends OBJECT records for a and b:
          deleting c rewrote the dnode block the three files share.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>send_cb</code>, dmu_send.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={SEND}
            from={1140}
            to={1146}
            symbol="send_cb"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 1140,
                to: 1142,
                text: `A hole is written as <a href="${L.holeFile}">a FREE of its range</a>, or, among the dnode blocks, as <a href="${L.holeMeta}">FREEOBJECTS</a>. <a href="${L.dumpFree}"><code>dump_free</code></a> merges a FREE into the one before it when it continues it, which is why the two holes of b travel as one record.`,
              },
              {
                from: 1143,
                to: 1146,
                text: `A data block becomes a WRITE with its data; REDACT belongs to redacted sends. A dnode becomes an OBJECT <a href="${L.dnodeRange}">a few lines above</a>, and <a href="${L.dnodeLoop}">every dnode of a visited dnode block</a> reaches this callback.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>A checksum that runs through the stream</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>dump_record</code> writes each record's header. Before it does, it carries a running fletcher-4
          checksum over the header and stores the value in the header's last 32 bytes. So the value in each record
          covers every byte of the stream before it.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>dump_record</code>, dmu_send.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={SEND}
            from={268}
            to={283}
            symbol="dump_record"
            notes={[
              { from: 268, to: 270, text: "The running sum is carried on over the header, up to its last 32 bytes." },
              {
                from: 271,
                to: 277,
                text: `Except for BEGIN, the sum goes into those 32 bytes, <code>drr_checksum</code>, which <a href="${L.overlay}">every record type has at the same place</a>.`,
              },
              {
                from: 281,
                to: 283,
                text: `Then the 32 bytes are summed, and <a href="${L.payloadSum}">the payload</a> after them. END carries <a href="${L.end}">the sum of the whole stream</a>. The figure shows the low 32 bits of each value.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>The receiver checks before it applies</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>receive_read_payload_and_next_header</code> keeps the same running sum on the receiving side and
          compares it with each header as it arrives. A record goes to the writer only once the header after it has
          been checked, so a record whose bytes were damaged is never applied.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>receive_read_payload_and_next_header</code>, dmu_recv.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={RECV}
            from={2861}
            to={2868}
            symbol="receive_read_payload_and_next_header"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 2861,
                to: 2866,
                text: `<code>drc_cksum</code> is the receiver's own sum: it <a href="${L.recvBegin}">starts over BEGIN</a> and has just been carried <a href="${L.recvSum}">over this header</a>. A mismatch stops the receive with <code>ECKSUM</code>. A checksum of all zeros is not checked.`,
              },
              {
                from: 2868,
                text: `The stored value is summed, as the sender did. Only then is the record before it <a href="${L.queue}">queued for the writer</a>. END is <a href="${L.endCheck}">checked once more</a>, and on an error <a href="${L.cleanup}">what the receive created is destroyed</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>A stream for a snapshot the receiver does not have</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          An incremental stream only makes sense on top of the snapshot it starts from. Its first record names that
          snapshot by its GUID, a number unique to each snapshot. <code>recv_begin_check_existing_impl</code> looks for
          it among the receiving filesystem's snapshots before anything is written.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>recv_begin_check_existing_impl</code>, dmu_recv.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={RECV}
            from={473}
            to={479}
            symbol="recv_begin_check_existing_impl"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 473,
                to: 477,
                text: `<a href="${L.loop}">The loop</a> goes from the newest snapshot to older ones until one has the stream's <code>fromguid</code>, which the sender <a href="${L.guids}">wrote in BEGIN</a>.`,
              },
              { from: 478, to: 479, text: "None has it: <code>ENODEV</code>, and nothing is received." },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>
      <SourcePanel lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figure leaves out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The figure is small: files of four 512-byte blocks under one indirect block, dnode blocks of four dnodes (a real
        16 KiB dnode block holds 32), a meta-dnode drawn with its two dnode blocks, no bonus buffer. The send runs as one
        sequence where the code joins several threads (traversal, merge, reader, the main thread) by queues that keep the
        order, and the receiver starts once the stream is complete, where the code receives through a pipe as the stream
        is written. Record headers are laid out as <code>dmu_replay_record_t</code>, with only the fields the figure
        uses filled in, and the receiver applies each record as <code>receive_process_record</code> dispatches it, by
        type. Redacted, raw (encrypted), compressed and resumable streams, embedded data, WRITE_BYREF and spill blocks
        are not shown; neither are throughput figures, which depend on the disks and the network, not on this code.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="send-receive" />
</Base>

